rocesses Login to view problem type\n\n## Paper Information\n\nPaper ID  | 2606.28869v1\nPublication Date  | 6/30/2026\nSource  | arXiv\nCategory  | math.PR\nSubcategory  | Rough paths theory, stochastic processes, mathematical finance\n\n### Authors\n\n  * Miquel Noguer i Alonso\n\n## Methodology\n\nName: Algebraic path theory with Hopf-algebraic and probabilistic constructions\n\nType: Theoretical mathematical framework development\n\nDescription: The paper develops a path-first theory using signatures as universal coordinates for deterministic paths, rough paths, jump streams, and path-valued random variables. The organizing principle is that geometricity is a first-order algebraic property whose obstructions are second-order (bracket for non-geometric lifts, covariance for averaging). Four central contributions are proved: (1) geometricity-defect theorem identifying quadratic covariation and coordinate covariance as canonical failures of shuffle multiplicativity; (2) Hopf square proving forward Itô signature equals iterated-sums signature and Marcus signature equals Hoffman's exponential image for pure-jump paths; (3) finite-dimensional linear closures for truncated expected signatures of affine and exponential Hawkes processes; (4) antisymmetric second-level cross-area detecting two-channel Hawkes excitation direction to first order. Numerical validation via Monte Carlo simulation with Ogata thinning confirms algebraic identities.\n\n### Domains\n\nMathematical probability theory Rough path theory Stochastic processes Financial event modeling Algebraic combinatorics\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\n### Limitations\n\n  * The geometricity-defect principle is a second-order characterization; higher-order obstructions are not addressed\n  * Hawkes closure results are specific to exponential kernels; general kernel Hawkes processes are not covered\n  * Local identifiability (Theorem 9.6) requires λ₀=µ and only works near T=0; global identifiability is open\n  * The cross-area directionality result is leading-order only; higher-order corrections are not characterized\n  * Affine closure (Theorem 9.1) is conditional on non-explosion, finite moments, and polynomial-domain closure\n  * Stable-law moment threshold (Theorem 12.1) limits raw expected signatures for heavy-tailed drivers\n  * The paper does not provide a public GitHub repository link for the reproducibility script\n  * Multivariate Hawkes identifiability beyond scalar local case remains conjectural (O3)\n\n### Future Work\n\n  * Signature-native graph recovery from cross-area sign patterns for sparse multivariate Hawkes kernels (Conjecture 15.1)\n  * Signature cumulants and Witt compression: solving Magnus-type recursion in O(Σℓd(k)) rather than O(d^N) operations (O1)\n  * Determinism modulus: Carnot distance from E[Sig(N)] to G(N) comparable to total coordinate standard deviation (O2)\n  * Finite-level multivariate Hawkes identifiability from level-two expected signatures (O3)\n  * Extension to non-exponential Hawkes kernels and general self-exciting processes\n  * Higher-order geometricity defects beyond second order--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ninteger partition — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/integer%20partition?page=2)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Ballantine--Beck--Feigon--Maurischat introduced the subsum polynomial \\[ \\operatorname{sp}(λ,x):=\\prod_i (1+x^{λ_i}) \\] attached to an integer partition $λ$, and studied rational functions obtained by summing reciprocals of these olynomials over natural classes of partitions. ... The approach is based on integer partitions of the total area, where each partition corresponds to a sequence of row lengths, and the product of all permutations of the parts accounts for all possible horizontal alignments of consecutive rows. ... Primc put forth three remarkable sets of conjectures, stating that the genera